{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 39, :comments ["<b>And you shall know this day.</b> One must understand what is special about knowing 'this day' compared to other days — what does he 'take to heart'? Or perhaps the intention is toward reminders needed to subdue the evil inclination that dwells in the heart of man." "<b>1)</b> This is according to what they said, of blessed memory (Berakhot 5a): 'If this filthy one encounters you,' etc., and if not, let him remember the day of death, and thereby his heart will be humbled. And this is the verse's saying <b>and you shall know</b> — he should place in his awareness the known day, which is the day of death, and thereby <b>and you shall take to your heart</b> — to return it to the good and upright path. And Scripture was precise in saying <b>לְבָבֶךָ</b> ('your heart') in plural form, to allude to the two inclinations — the good inclination and the evil inclination:" "<b>2)</b> This is according to what they said, of blessed memory (Sukkah 52b), that in the future to come the Holy One, blessed be He, will bring the Satan and slaughter him before all of Israel. And Moses instructed that a person should place in his awareness that great day on which HaShem will draw His sword and slaughter Samael the wicked, and thereby he will return to his heart to the upright path, for the Satan will be humbled before him." "Further, it intends that he remember that great day, which is the Day of Judgment — what will he do when El arises and when He visits, what will he answer Him? And how great will be his shame before the assembly and congregation, supernal and infernal, and thereby 'and you shall take,' etc." "<b>4)</b> This is according to what they said, of blessed memory (Bava Batra 84a): Why does the sun in the morning have a red appearance? etc. — because it passes over the entrance of Gehinnom, etc. And this is the verse's saying <b>and you shall know</b> — set your mind to know the deed of the day, and you will see evidence in the sun that there is judgment for those who transgress His will, and thereby you shall take to your heart, etc." "<b>(5)</b> Along the lines of what the men of truth say concerning שו״ע, that the dimension of holiness is called day, and the dimension of estrangement is called night, and through a person's deeds the dimension of day may be lost and his portion become in the measure of night. And therefore David prayed and said ״מִפַּחַד בַּלֵּילוֹת״ (Psalms 91:5). And this is what the verse means by <b>וְיָדַעְתָּ הַיּוֹם</b> — meaning, strive to know this quality called the day, and through this you yourself will turn your heart to the correct path." "<b>(6)</b> Along the lines of what they said, may their memory be for blessing, in the Zohar (vol. 3, 178a), and this is its language: מַאן אִנּוּן מָארֵי חוּשְׁבָּנָא? אִנּוּן דְּבָכָל לֵילְיָא עָבְדִין חוּשְׁבָּנָא בְּנַפְשַיְיהוּ בְּמָה דְּעָבְדִין בְּהַהוּא יוֹמָא וּמְתַקְּנִין אָרְחַיְיהוּ, etc., end quote. Now, if a person does this thing day by day, he will feel his evil deeds and turn back from the unintentional sins and confess over what has passed. But otherwise, due to the scantness of his feeling about them, he will persist in doing such deeds and greater than them, and even if he repents he will not remember what has befallen him to confess over them. And therefore Moses commanded with the statement <b>וְיָדַעְתָּ הַיּוֹם</b> — meaning, every day he should examine the deed of that day, and this is the way of the masters of reckoning (מָארֵי דְחוּשְׁבָּנָא). And through this <b>וַהֲשֵׁבֹתָ אֶל לְבָבֶךָ</b> — that he not persist in them, and needless to say that he not add further evil." "<b>(7)</b> Along the lines of what they said in the Zohar (Zohar vol. 1, 233b), that on the day the soul comes from the supernal world, HaShem adjures it concerning the Torah and warns it with many punishments and many promises if you do good — and see in the Zohar. Corresponding to this day He said <b>וְיָדְתָּ הַיּוֹם</b> — meaning, the day that it journeyed: that one should remember the day of the departure of his soul from the supernal world with all its admonitions, and this will return to his heart, etc." "<b>(8)</b> Along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Ḥagigah 12a), that this world is called night and the supernal world is called day. Now, since a person sees contrary things in this world — a righteous person for whom it is bad, a wicked person for whom it is good — beyond searching, his heart may turn him from choosing the eternal good, and the wicked people he sees in this world who flourish and prosper may say to him, Do you not see that it is good? Therefore He said <b>וְיָדַעְתָּ הַיּוֹם</b> — which is the world that is called day — <b>וַהֲשֵׁבֹתָ אֶל לְבָבֶךָ</b> an eternal turning, that HaShem chooses existence for His beloved and repays His enemies, etc." "<b>(9)</b> Along the lines of what they said, may their memory be for blessing (Shabbat 88a), regarding the verse (Genesis 1:31) ״וַיְהִי עֶרֶב וגו׳ יוֹם הַשִּׁשִּׁי״ — that HaShem stipulated with the work of Creation upon the day of the giving of the Torah, etc. And this is what He hinted by saying <b>וְיָדַעְתָּ הַיּוֹם</b> — meaning, the great day that was revealed at Mount Sinai, how awe-inspiring are His deeds, and upon which the stipulation with the world was made. And through this you shall return your heart to the path of Torah. And along this line, what He said, <b>כִּי ה׳ הוּא הָאֱלֹהִים</b>, follows from what precedes it, ״וַהֲשֵׁבֹתָ אֶל לְבָבֶךָ״ — for through that day it was made known to all the world that there is no Elohim but Him in the heavens, etc., and on the earth. As they said, may their memory be for blessing (Mekhilta, parashat Anokhi), regarding the verse (Isaiah 48:16) ״לֹא מֵרֹאשׁ בַּסֵּתֶר דִּבַּרְתִּי״." "<b>(j)</b> It may be explained along the path of what they said, of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 25:12), that Shabbat is weighed against the entire Torah. Now, since the evil impulse comes and says to a person: ״What is your strength, that you hope to fulfill the entire Torah, which is a person's perfection?״ — and even more so a person whom many evils have overtaken, the evil impulse distances him, saying: ״What can you fix of what your hands have wrought through evils?״ — therefore Moses said <b>and you shall know the day</b>: that which is written about it as ״the day,״ which is Shabbat, as it is written (Exodus 16:25): ״כִּי שַׁבָּת הַיּוֹם״ — direct your mind to it, and through it you shall restore to your heart that it is weighed against the entire Torah. And this will be useful to demolish both arguments: both to repair what he has transgressed of the rebellions, and for the perfection of the soul, for through its observance it is as though he has fulfilled all 613 commandments."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:15:46.078387809Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}